WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.560
SMRITI PRAMANICK: My
name is Smriti Pramanick,

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.601
and today, I'd love to
tell you a little bit more

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:06.090
about what I've been
working on this past year.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:09.540
So if you've ever been
to a classical concert,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.150
you're probably familiar
with a music program.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:15.570
It's usually paper and includes
the pieces that will be played,

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.390
the composer, and maybe
some background info.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:21.750
The lab I'm working with, The
Music Technology Lab at MIT,

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.450
is trying to revolutionize
the concert-going experience

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:28.830
and provide a new interactive
program called ConcertCue.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.680
So what happens is a
concert concertgoer

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.270
is listening to the
piece and provided

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.779
relevant pieces of information
as the piece progresses.

00:00:36.779 --> 00:00:39.070
So in the beginning, they
might receive some background

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.650
information, and then
halfway through the piece,

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:45.130
they might get their attention
drawn to some repeating melody,

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:49.840
or be informed that some
trumpet section is coming up.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.150
So the way this
works is a technique

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.040
called audio
synchronization where

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.280
we have a reference recording
in the database, which

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.340
has been annotated with beats
and relevant information.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:02.950
And currently, a human operator
that sits there and taps

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:05.390
out the beats, in
the live setting.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.890
So my project focused on
automating this process

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:12.220
and figuring out how we can take
in the live audio in a concert

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.850
setting and have the computer
tell us which beat we're on.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:19.450
So there are a couple
of different approaches

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:20.320
to this problem.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.420
And they all involve
online variants

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.610
of a audio
synchronization algorithm

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.680
called dynamic time warping.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.410
So I surveyed many
of these algorithms

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.000
and decided to implement them.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.460
They were mostly theoretical
before, so implementing them

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.450
was a lot of fun and took
some work to actually make

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.920
it work in a live setting.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.320
And then I got the
opportunity to try out

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.170
one of these algorithms at
the Boston Symphony Orchestra

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.380
a couple of weeks ago, with
very promising results.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.980
Essentially, the beat that
the computer predicted

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.440
we were on in the life
piece matched perfectly

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.040
with the human operator.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.885
So it looks like this
will be able to work well

00:01:57.885 --> 00:01:58.760
with the application.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:02.820
And I look forward to seeing
that integration happen.

